<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 17: Spacetime Diagrams</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> Reading covers sections 5.1 through 5.7. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Draw the worldline of a particle moving at constant speed on a spacetime diagram. Use your drawing to show 
                that the slope of the worldline is <m>c/v</m> or, if you're using SR units, <m>1/v</m>. Note: I will
                use SR units exclusively this week. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Describe the motion of the objects that follow the worldlines below (image credit: Prof. 
            Dan V. Schroeder). Note that he labels the axes as x and t, which is the usual practice, but we're assuming
            that either x is measured in seconds or t in meters. Be quantitative: if you can determine a 
            particle speed using the grid lines, do it.
        </p>

        <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz17/SpacetimeDiagramWithFiveWorldlines.svg" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>Convert these pdf images to a different format man</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Explain the following statement about spacetime diagrams: “The time axis of a spacetime diagram is the 
            worldline of an observer present at the origin event and at rest”.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		The picture below (also from Prof. Schroeder) shows a geometric method of determining an observer's 
                space axis, that is, the set of events that are simultaneous to a particular origin event <m>O</m>.
                Event <m>O</m> is taken to be half-way in time between <m>F</m> and <m>S</m>, and then the line 
                <m>OB</m> gives you the space axis. Draw events <m>F,O,S,B</m> using the <m>x</m> and <m>t</m> 
                axes of an observer moving to the left relative to the t<prime/>x<prime/> observer (so the 
                t<prime/>x<prime/> observer is moving to the right in the x-t diagram). Use this to qualitatively 
                draw the t<prime/> and x<prime/> axes in the x-t diagram.
        </p>

        <image source="images\quiz-images\quiz17\BouncingLightPulse1.svg" width="50%">
            <shortdescription></shortdescription>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>